# Expectation record for 09_optimization_levels/008_folded_vs_runtime_equivalence.c
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 008_folded_vs_runtime_equivalence
area               = 09_optimization_levels
description        = Keystone: the same computation folded from literals and computed from volatile operands must agree at -O0, -O1 and -O2
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
Free of undefined and unspecified behaviour. No signed overflow: 191 * 7 is 1337, 13 << 2 is
52 on a positive signed operand well inside range, 100 / 3 is 33, and the sum 1356 is then
reduced modulo 97. In MIX_WIDE both literals carry the LL suffix so 1234567 * 89101 is
performed in long long, giving 110001154267, far inside 64-bit range. Shift counts are 2 and
3, strictly in range, and the right shift operates on an unsigned operand so no
implementation-defined signed-shift question arises. The only divisor is 3 and the only
modulus is 97, so there is no division by zero and no INT_MIN over -1 case. The addition
inside MIX_UNS wraps modulo 2 to the 32, which is well defined for unsigned int and is not
overflow. No aliasing violation, no read of uninitialized storage, no pointer arithmetic, and
no object modified twice between sequence points. Every macro parameter appears exactly once
in every macro body, so each argument is evaluated exactly once and no operand is duplicated.
No macro argument has a side effect, so there are zero side-effecting argument expressions.
No dependence on padding bytes or on relative addresses.
Several volatile objects are read within a single expression; their relative order is
unspecified, but no volatile object is modified anywhere in the program, so every read of a
given object yields the same value and the printed output does not depend on the ordering.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
No implementation-defined behaviour is observed. Widths are normalized: only int, unsigned int
and long long are used, all of fixed width on all four targets, and long is never used because
sizeof(long) is 4 on i686 and 8 on the other three targets. The wide values carry LL suffixes
and are printed with %lld because 109013499946 exceeds 32 bits. Plain char is not used, so the
measured plain-char signedness difference (signed on x86-64 and i686, unsigned on AArch64 and
RISC-V 64) cannot be observed. No address, pointer value, sizeof or _Alignof result is printed.
No floating point is used. The modular wraparound inside MIX_UNS is required behaviour for
unsigned int, not an implementation-defined property, so it is identical on every target.
Each computation is written exactly once as a macro and instantiated twice, once from literal
constants and once from volatile operands, which guarantees by construction that the two
variants are the same computation. How much of the folded variant each compiler evaluates at
compile time is at its discretion; the two printed columns must agree regardless.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
int  folded=95 runtime=95 equal=1
uns  folded=8043744 runtime=8043744 equal=1
wide folded=109013499946 runtime=109013499946 equal=1
rel  folded=20 runtime=20 equal=1
cond folded=24 runtime=24 equal=1
all_equal=1
END
